/* The Art of War and Peace: figures, landscape works and texts, word for word from the artist.
   Images are files in awp/img (the map below says which). Read by awp/engine.js. */
window.__AWP__ = {"theta0":0.62,"bandF":[0.17,0.35,0.53,0.71],"pairs":[],"figures":[{"id":"alexander","name":"Alexander the Great","dates":"356 BC – 323 BC","u":0.0,"band":1,"teaser":"Can conquest ever be separated from creation, or are they forever bound together?","text":["Born in Pella, the capital of Macedon, Alexander III inherited not only a kingdom from his father, King Philip II, but also a disciplined army and an unfulfilled ambition to conquer Persia. Tutored by Aristotle, he grew up steeped in philosophy, science, and Homeric legend, particularly the <em>Iliad</em>, which he reportedly carried with him on campaign. When he became king in 336 BC, he moved quickly to secure Greece before turning east. Over the next decade, his campaigns became the stuff of legend. He defeated King Darius III of Persia at the battles of Issus in 333 BC and Gaugamela in 331 BC, victories that shattered the Persian Empire and opened the path to Egypt and beyond. In Egypt, he founded Alexandria, one of many cities bearing his name, symbols of both conquest and cultural ambition.","Alexander’s campaigns stretched as far as the Indus River, where his weary troops finally refused to march further. His methods of war were often ruthless, as seen in the destruction of Thebes early in his reign and the siege of Tyre, yet he also sought to merge cultures, adopting Persian customs and encouraging intermarriage between his soldiers and local populations. His legacy is a paradox. He spread Greek language and ideas across three continents, creating what historians call the Hellenistic world, yet he did so through relentless warfare. Later leaders, including Napoleon Bonaparte, would consciously model themselves on his example. Alexander’s life raises an enduring question that echoes through this installation. Can conquest ever be separated from creation, or are they forever bound together?"],"kind":"person","p":[-0.6834,-0.7444,-0.0111]},{"id":"napoleon","name":"Napoleon Bonaparte","dates":"August 15, 1769 – May 5, 1821","u":0.5,"band":1,"teaser":"Like Alexander before him, he combined innovation with destruction.","text":["Napoleon Bonaparte rose from relative obscurity during the French Revolution to become one of history’s most formidable military leaders. His early victories in Italy and Egypt showcased his strategic brilliance, and in 1804 he crowned himself Emperor of the French. Over the next decade, his campaigns redrew the map of Europe, spreading revolutionary ideals even as he consolidated personal power.","Battles such as Austerlitz in 1805 demonstrated his tactical genius, while his disastrous invasion of Russia in 1812 revealed the limits of ambition. Ultimately defeated at Waterloo in 1815, Napoleon was exiled, his empire undone. Like Alexander before him, he combined innovation with destruction. His legacy lives on in legal reforms such as the Napoleonic Code, even as his wars left millions dead."],"kind":"person","p":[0.995,-0.0013,0.1913]},{"id":"gandhi","name":"Mahatma Gandhi","dates":"October 2, 1869 – January 30, 1948","u":0.125,"band":2,"teaser":"The refusal to strike back can itself become a form of strength.","text":["Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi was trained as a lawyer in London, but it was in South Africa that he first began to develop the philosophy that would define his life. Confronted with racial discrimination, he organized campaigns of civil resistance rooted in satyagraha, or truth-force, a method that rejected violence while refusing submission. When he returned to India, he brought this philosophy into the struggle against British colonial rule, transforming what might have been a conventional political movement into a moral force.","Gandhi led nationwide campaigns such as the Non-Cooperation Movement and the Salt March of 1930, in which he and his followers defied British law by producing salt from the sea. His approach inspired millions, yet it also demanded discipline and sacrifice. He believed that means and ends were inseparable, that true peace could not be born from violence. His influence extended far beyond India, shaping leaders like Martin Luther King Jr. and Nelson Mandela. Gandhi’s life suggests that resistance can be both powerful and restrained, that the refusal to strike back can itself become a form of strength."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.3264,-0.0253,-0.935]},{"id":"mlk","name":"Martin Luther King Jr.","dates":"January 15, 1929 – April 4, 1968","u":0.625,"band":2,"teaser":"Justice achieved through peace carries a strength that violence cannot sustain.","text":["Martin Luther King Jr. emerged as a central figure in the American civil rights movement, drawing deeply from the philosophy of nonviolence developed by Mahatma Gandhi. As a Baptist minister, his leadership during events such as the Montgomery Bus Boycott and the Birmingham Campaign demonstrated the power of peaceful resistance in the face of systemic injustice.","King’s speeches, particularly “I Have a Dream,” framed the struggle for civil rights as part of a broader moral arc. He also spoke out against the Vietnam War, linking racial injustice at home with violence abroad. His assassination in 1968 marked a profound loss, yet his influence endures. King represents the belief that justice achieved through peace carries a strength that violence cannot sustain."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.9727,-0.0285,0.325]},{"id":"lincoln","name":"Abraham Lincoln","dates":"February 12, 1809 – April 14, 1865","u":0.25,"band":1,"teaser":"War becomes a means toward a more just peace.","text":["Abraham Lincoln rose from humble beginnings to lead the United States through its greatest internal crisis. Elected president in 1860, he faced the secession of Southern states and the outbreak of the Civil War. Lincoln approached the conflict with a clear sense of purpose, preserving the Union while gradually redefining the war as a fight against slavery. The Emancipation Proclamation of 1863 marked a turning point, transforming the moral stakes of the war.","Lincoln’s leadership required balancing firmness with empathy. His speeches, including the Gettysburg Address, framed the war as a test of the nation’s founding ideals. He worked closely with generals such as Ulysses S. Grant to bring the conflict to an end. Just days after the Confederate surrender, Lincoln was assassinated, his vision for reconciliation left unfinished. He remains a symbol of leadership under immense strain, where war becomes a means toward a more just peace."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.8397,-0.5402,-0.1535]},{"id":"grant","name":"Ulysses S. Grant","dates":"April 27, 1822 – July 23, 1885","u":0.75,"band":1,"teaser":"The difficulty of sustaining peace once the fighting ends.","text":["Ulysses S. Grant began the American Civil War as a relatively unknown officer, but his determination and strategic clarity quickly set him apart. Rising through the ranks of the Union Army, he secured key victories at Fort Donelson and Vicksburg, the latter giving the Union control of the Mississippi River. As commanding general, Grant coordinated a series of relentless campaigns against Robert E. Lee, culminating in Lee’s surrender at Appomattox Court House in 1865.","Grant understood that ending the war required not only victory but reconciliation. The terms he offered Lee were generous, allowing Confederate soldiers to return home rather than face punishment. As president, he worked to enforce civil rights during Reconstruction, though his administration faced significant challenges. Grant’s life reflects the transition from war to peace, and the difficulty of sustaining that peace once the fighting ends."],"kind":"person","p":[0.039,-0.1458,-0.9862]},{"id":"hitler","name":"Adolf Hitler","dates":"April 20, 1889 – April 30, 1945","u":0.2917,"band":2,"teaser":"Where ideology and power combined to produce destruction on an unimaginable scale.","text":["Adolf Hitler was born in Austria and came of age during a period of instability in Europe. After serving as a soldier in World War I, he entered German politics, joining the National Socialist German Workers’ Party. Through a combination of oratory, propaganda, and political maneuvering, he rose to power in 1933, becoming Chancellor and soon consolidating control as dictator. His regime was built on extreme nationalism, racial ideology, and the systematic persecution of those he deemed undesirable.","Hitler’s expansionist ambitions led directly to World War II, beginning with the invasion of Poland in 1939. The war spread across continents, drawing in nations around the globe. At the same time, his government carried out the Holocaust, the genocide of six million Jews and millions of others. His leadership represents one of the darkest chapters in human history, where ideology and power combined to produce destruction on an unimaginable scale. His defeat in 1945 came only after immense sacrifice, leaving a legacy that continues to define discussions of war, morality, and human capacity for both cruelty and resistance."],"kind":"person","p":[0.4411,-0.3193,0.8435]},{"id":"stalin","name":"Joseph Stalin","dates":"December 18, 1878 – March 5, 1953","u":0.7917,"band":2,"teaser":"A leader who helped defeat one of history’s greatest threats while perpetuating terror within his own nation.","text":["Joseph Stalin rose from revolutionary beginnings to become the leader of the Soviet Union after the death of Vladimir Lenin. His rule was marked by rapid industrialization and collectivization, but also by purges, forced labor camps, and widespread repression. Millions suffered under his policies, particularly during the Great Purge of the 1930s.","During World War II, Stalin led the Soviet Union against Nazi Germany, playing a crucial role in the defeat of Adolf Hitler. Battles such as Stalingrad became turning points in the war. Yet victory came at immense human cost. Stalin’s legacy is one of contradiction, a leader who helped defeat one of history’s greatest threats while perpetuating terror within his own nation."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.884,-0.1553,-0.4361]},{"id":"genghis","name":"Genghis Khan","dates":"c. 1162 – August 1227","u":0.0625,"band":0,"teaser":"A testament to the dual nature of conquest.","text":["Born Temüjin on the Mongolian steppe, Genghis Khan rose from a childhood of hardship to unite the Mongol tribes. Through a combination of military innovation, discipline, and strategic alliances, he created the largest contiguous empire in history. His campaigns swept across Asia and into Europe, marked by both devastating destruction and unprecedented connectivity.","Cities that resisted were often annihilated, yet his empire also facilitated trade along the Silk Road, linking distant cultures. His legacy is both feared and admired, a testament to the dual nature of conquest. Like Alexander, he reshaped the world through force, leaving behind a legacy that continues to influence global history."],"kind":"person","p":[0.3761,-0.6812,-0.6448]},{"id":"caesar","name":"Julius Caesar","dates":"July 12 or 13, 100 BC – March 15, 44 BC","u":0.1875,"band":0,"teaser":"Power, once concentrated, can destabilize the very systems it seeks to control.","text":["Gaius Julius Caesar rose through the ranks of Roman politics and military command during a period of instability. His conquest of Gaul, chronicled in his own writings, expanded Rome’s territory and enhanced his reputation. Yet his growing power brought him into conflict with the Roman Senate, leading to civil war. His crossing of the Rubicon River in 49 BC marked a decisive break with tradition and the beginning of a new era.","After defeating his rivals, Caesar became dictator, implementing reforms that reshaped Roman governance. His rule, however, alarmed those who feared the end of the Republic. In 44 BC, he was assassinated by a group of senators, an act intended to restore balance but which instead plunged Rome into further conflict. Caesar’s life illustrates how power, once concentrated, can destabilize the very systems it seeks to control."],"kind":"person","p":[0.0884,0.1089,-0.9968]},{"id":"urban","name":"Pope Urban II","dates":"c. 1035 – July 29, 1099","u":0.3125,"band":0,"teaser":"He stands at the intersection of faith and force.","text":["Born Odo of Châtillon in France, Pope Urban II rose through the ranks of the medieval Church during a time of political fragmentation and religious fervor. His papacy, which began in 1088, was marked by efforts to strengthen the authority of the Church across Europe. In 1095, at the Council of Clermont, he delivered a sermon that would alter the course of history. Calling upon knights and nobles to aid Christians in the Holy Land, he urged them to take up arms and reclaim Jerusalem from Muslim control. His words, reportedly met with cries of “Deus vult,” or “God wills it,” ignited the First Crusade.","The Crusaders who answered his call embarked on a brutal journey marked by both devotion and violence. By 1099, they captured Jerusalem, but the victory came with widespread slaughter. Urban himself did not live to see the outcome, yet his influence reshaped relations between Christianity and Islam for centuries. His legacy is a reminder of how belief can be harnessed to justify war, transforming spiritual conviction into military action. In the context of this installation, he stands at the intersection of faith and force, where ideals can inspire both compassion and conflict."],"kind":"person","p":[0.5142,-0.0035,0.8655]},{"id":"jesus","name":"Jesus Christ","dates":"c. 6–4 BC – c. 30–33 AD","u":0.4375,"band":0,"teaser":"He represents the enduring tension between spiritual ideals and human action.","text":["Jesus of Nazareth lived in a time of Roman occupation and social unrest. As a teacher and preacher, he spoke of love, forgiveness, and the coming of a kingdom not defined by earthly power. His message challenged both religious authorities and imperial structures, emphasizing compassion over domination. His teachings, preserved in the Gospels, include the Sermon on the Mount, where he urged followers to turn the other cheek and love their enemies.","Executed by crucifixion, a method reserved for those seen as threats to the state, Jesus’s death became central to the faith that followed. Christianity spread across the Roman Empire and beyond, shaping cultures and histories for centuries. His message has inspired movements for peace, even as it has been invoked in times of conflict. He represents the enduring tension between spiritual ideals and human action."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.5142,0.0019,-0.8497]},{"id":"buddha","name":"Buddha — Siddhartha Gautama","dates":"c. 6th–5th century BC","u":0.5625,"band":0,"teaser":"A quiet counterbalance to the noise of conflict.","text":["Siddhartha Gautama was born into a life of privilege, shielded from suffering by his royal family. Yet when he encountered the realities of illness, aging, and death, he renounced his status and set out in search of understanding. After years of ascetic practice and meditation, he attained enlightenment beneath the Bodhi tree, becoming the Buddha, or “the awakened one.”","His teachings centered on the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path, offering a way to end suffering through ethical living, mindfulness, and wisdom. Unlike many figures in this installation, the Buddha did not engage in war or governance. His influence lies in the realm of inner transformation. Yet his ideas have shaped entire civilizations, offering an alternative vision in which peace begins within the individual and radiates outward. In the context of <em>The Art of War and Peace</em>, he stands as a quiet counterbalance to the noise of conflict."],"kind":"person","p":[0.9226,-0.0133,-0.4265]},{"id":"washington","name":"George Washington","dates":"February 22, 1732 – December 14, 1799","u":0.6875,"band":0,"teaser":"Victory can give way to stability when guided by principle.","text":["George Washington’s life is closely tied to the founding of the United States. As commander of the Continental Army during the American Revolutionary War, he led colonial forces against the British Empire, enduring years of hardship before achieving victory at Yorktown in 1781. His leadership combined persistence with restraint, holding together an army that often lacked resources and support.","After the war, Washington could have claimed power, yet he chose a different path. As the first president, he set precedents that shaped the nation’s future, including the peaceful transfer of power. His decision to step down after two terms established a model of leadership grounded in service rather than control. Washington’s legacy bridges war and peace, illustrating how victory can give way to stability when guided by principle."],"kind":"person","p":[0.9748,-0.2377,-0.1772]},{"id":"garibaldi","name":"Giuseppe Garibaldi","dates":"July 4, 1807 – June 2, 1882","u":0.8125,"band":0,"teaser":"Between the desire to fight for change and the need to build lasting peace.","text":["Giuseppe Garibaldi was a central figure in the unification of Italy, a revolutionary whose life was defined by action. Known as the “Hero of Two Worlds,” he fought in conflicts across South America before returning to Italy to lead volunteer forces in the struggle for national unity. His Expedition of the Thousand in 1860, in which he and a small band of followers captured Sicily and Naples, became one of the most dramatic episodes of the Risorgimento.","Garibaldi’s leadership combined charisma with a deep commitment to republican ideals. Though he achieved military success, he ultimately ceded power to unify Italy under a monarchy, placing national unity above personal ambition. His story reflects the tension between revolution and stability, between the desire to fight for change and the need to build lasting peace."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.5518,-0.6822,0.473]},{"id":"redcloud","name":"Red Cloud","dates":"1822 – December 10, 1909","u":0.9375,"band":0,"teaser":"The fierce defense of homeland and the painful realities of survival.","text":["Red Cloud, born near the Platte River, emerged as one of the most formidable leaders of the Oglala Lakota during a period of intense conflict between Native nations and the expanding United States. His leadership came to prominence during Red Cloud’s War from 1866 to 1868, a conflict sparked by the construction of the Bozeman Trail through Lakota territory. Unlike many Indigenous leaders, Red Cloud achieved a decisive military victory. His forces successfully harassed and defeated U.S. troops, most notably in the Fetterman Fight of 1866, where an entire detachment of soldiers was killed.","The resulting Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868 temporarily secured Lakota control over the Powder River Country and led to the abandonment of U.S. forts in the region. Yet Red Cloud understood that military victory alone could not ensure survival. In later years, he became a diplomat, traveling to Washington, D.C., and advocating for his people through negotiation rather than war. His life reflects a transition that many Indigenous leaders were forced to make, from resistance to reluctant accommodation. He represents both the fierce defense of homeland and the painful realities of survival in the face of overwhelming change."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.7259,-0.6219,0.3121]},{"id":"lee","name":"Robert E. Lee","dates":"January 19, 1807 – October 12, 1870","u":0.0417,"band":2,"teaser":"Reflecting the complexities of memory, honor, and justice.","text":["Robert E. Lee was a career military officer who chose to align with his home state of Virginia when it seceded from the Union. As commander of the Confederate Army of Northern Virginia, he became known for his tactical skill and bold maneuvers, achieving significant victories against larger Union forces. Battles such as Chancellorsville demonstrated his ability to take calculated risks.","Yet his leadership was tied to a cause rooted in the preservation of slavery. His defeat by Ulysses S. Grant marked the collapse of the Confederacy. After the war, Lee encouraged reconciliation, urging former Confederates to accept the outcome and rebuild. His legacy remains deeply contested, reflecting the complexities of memory, honor, and justice."],"kind":"person","p":[0.9308,-0.3577,0.1714]},{"id":"jackson","name":"Stonewall Jackson","dates":"January 21, 1824 – May 10, 1863","u":0.2083,"band":2,"teaser":"Courage and conviction entwined with a cause history would ultimately condemn.","text":["Thomas Jonathan “Stonewall” Jackson earned his famous nickname during the First Battle of Bull Run in 1861, when his brigade stood firm under heavy Union attack. A deeply religious and intensely disciplined military commander, Jackson became one of the Confederacy’s most respected generals during the American Civil War. Serving under Robert E. Lee, he played a crucial role in campaigns throughout Virginia, including the Shenandoah Valley Campaign and the Battle of Chancellorsville, where his daring flanking maneuver stunned Union forces.","Jackson’s brilliance on the battlefield made him legendary, yet his legacy cannot be separated from the cause he served. The Confederacy fought to preserve a system rooted in slavery, placing Jackson among the many historical figures whose personal qualities exist alongside morally troubling realities. In 1863, he was accidentally wounded by his own troops and later died from complications, a loss that deeply affected Lee and the Confederate war effort. Jackson represents the tragic duality of war, courage and conviction entwined with a cause history would ultimately condemn."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.8917,-0.4894,0.1293]},{"id":"douglass","name":"Frederick Douglass","dates":"c. February 1818 – February 20, 1895","u":0.4583,"band":2,"teaser":"Words can be as powerful as weapons, shaping the moral direction of a nation in conflict.","text":["Born into slavery in Maryland, Frederick Douglass escaped in 1838 and transformed himself into one of the most powerful voices of the nineteenth century. His autobiography, <em>Narrative of the Life of Frederick Douglass</em>, exposed the brutal realities of slavery to a wide audience, becoming a foundational text in the abolitionist movement. Douglass possessed an extraordinary command of language, using speeches and writing to argue not only for the end of slavery but for full equality.","During the American Civil War, Douglass advised Abraham Lincoln and pushed for the enlistment of Black soldiers in the Union Army. He understood that the war was not simply a political struggle, but a moral reckoning. After the war, he continued to advocate for civil rights and women’s suffrage. Douglass’s life illustrates that words can be as powerful as weapons, shaping the moral direction of a nation in conflict."],"kind":"person","p":[0.8128,0.0618,-0.6224]},{"id":"tubman","name":"Harriet Tubman","dates":"c. March 1822 – March 10, 1913","u":0.375,"band":2,"teaser":"She represents the quiet, relentless force of liberation.","text":["Harriet Tubman was born into slavery in Maryland, where violence and hardship defined her early life. After escaping in 1849, she made a choice that set her apart in history. She returned. Again and again, she traveled back into slaveholding territory, guiding dozens of enslaved people to freedom along the Underground Railroad. Known as “Moses” to those she led, Tubman relied on an intricate network of safe houses, allies, and her own unshakable resolve. The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 made her work even more dangerous, yet she never lost a passenger.","During the American Civil War, Tubman served the Union cause as a nurse, scout, and spy. In 1863, she helped lead the Combahee River Raid in South Carolina, which liberated more than 700 enslaved people. Her life blurred the line between resistance and war, between compassion and strategy. Tubman carried no formal rank, yet her courage altered lives in ways that echo beyond any battlefield. She represents the quiet, relentless force of liberation."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.4629,-0.6991,-0.5588]},{"id":"mandela","name":"Nelson Mandela","dates":"July 18, 1918 – December 5, 2013","u":0.5417,"band":2,"teaser":"The greatest victories are won in the ability to transform anger into unity and justice into peace.","text":["Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born into the Thembu royal family in rural South Africa, yet his life would be defined not by inherited status but by resistance. As a young lawyer in Johannesburg, he became involved with the African National Congress in the 1940s, at a time when apartheid was tightening its grip. Initially committed to nonviolent protest, Mandela helped organize campaigns of civil disobedience. However, after the Sharpeville Massacre in 1960 and the banning of opposition groups, he co-founded Umkhonto we Sizwe, an armed wing that carried out sabotage against government infrastructure. This shift marked a turning point, illustrating the difficult line between peaceful resistance and armed struggle.","In 1964, Mandela was sentenced to life imprisonment and spent 27 years behind bars, much of it on Robben Island. During those years, he became a global symbol of resistance to injustice. When he was finally released in 1990, South Africa stood on the brink of civil war. Mandela chose negotiation over retaliation, working with President F.W. de Klerk to dismantle apartheid. Elected president in 1994, he led the nation through reconciliation efforts, most notably the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, which sought accountability without vengeance. Mandela’s story stands in profound contrast to figures who ruled through fear. His life suggests that the greatest victories are not won on battlefields, but in the ability to transform anger into unity and justice into peace."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.0832,-0.7185,0.6695]},{"id":"churchill","name":"Winston Churchill","dates":"November 30, 1874 – January 24, 1965","u":0.7083,"band":2,"teaser":"A figure of resilience in war, but also a reminder that victory does not erase complexity.","text":["Born into British aristocracy, Winston Churchill lived a life that seemed almost designed for history. Before entering politics, he was a soldier and war correspondent, witnessing conflict firsthand in Cuba, India, and Sudan. His early career included both triumphs and failures, most notably the disastrous Gallipoli campaign during World War I, which temporarily derailed his political standing. Yet it was in Britain’s darkest hour, in 1940, that Churchill rose to define his legacy. As Prime Minister during World War II, he refused to negotiate with Adolf Hitler, even when invasion seemed imminent.","Churchill’s speeches, delivered with defiant resolve, rallied a nation under siege. “We shall fight on the beaches” became more than rhetoric; it was a psychological line of defense. Under his leadership, Britain endured the Blitz and remained a crucial force in the Allied victory. Yet his legacy is layered. He was also a staunch defender of the British Empire, and his policies have been criticized for their impact on colonial populations. Churchill stands as a figure of resilience in war, but also a reminder that victory does not erase complexity."],"kind":"person","p":[0.5425,-0.7124,-0.4138]},{"id":"mussolini","name":"Benito Mussolini","dates":"July 29, 1883 – April 28, 1945","u":0.875,"band":2,"teaser":"The rise of authoritarianism fueled by fear and pride, and its eventual collapse.","text":["Benito Mussolini began his career as a socialist journalist before transforming into the founder of fascism. In 1922, he marched on Rome and established a dictatorship that promised order and national revival. His regime emphasized militarism, nationalism, and absolute loyalty to the state. Mussolini sought to recreate the glory of the Roman Empire, invading Ethiopia in 1935 and aligning Italy with Nazi Germany under Adolf Hitler.","His ambitions led Italy into World War II, where military failures and domestic dissatisfaction weakened his hold on power. By 1943, he was overthrown, and in 1945, he was captured and executed by Italian partisans. Mussolini’s life charts the rise of authoritarianism fueled by fear and pride, and its eventual collapse under the weight of its own violence."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.1179,-0.7362,-0.6571]},{"id":"tojo","name":"Hideki Tojo","dates":"December 30, 1884 – December 23, 1948","u":0.9583,"band":2,"teaser":"How national policy and military action can converge with devastating consequences.","text":["Hideki Tojo was a military officer who became Prime Minister of Japan during World War II, a period in which the country pursued aggressive expansion across Asia and the Pacific. As a leading figure in Japan’s wartime government, he played a central role in decisions that led to the attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941, bringing the United States into the conflict.","Under his leadership, Japan engaged in campaigns marked by both strategic ambition and significant human cost. Following Japan’s defeat, Tojo was arrested, tried for war crimes, and executed in 1948. His legacy is tied to the broader story of World War II in the Pacific, reflecting how national policy and military action can converge with devastating consequences."],"kind":"person","p":[0.6335,-0.3803,-0.6931]},{"id":"tolstoy","name":"Leo Tolstoy","dates":"September 9, 1828 – November 20, 1910","u":0.0,"band":3,"teaser":"Behind every war are countless personal stories of loss, love, and survival.","text":["Leo Tolstoy was born into Russian aristocracy but spent much of his life questioning the structures of power and violence around him. His novel <em>War and Peace</em>, set during the Napoleonic Wars, is not simply a story of battles and strategy, but a meditation on the lives of individuals caught within vast historical forces. Tolstoy himself served in the Crimean War, an experience that deeply shaped his views on the futility of conflict.","In his later years, he became a moral philosopher, advocating for nonviolence, simplicity, and spiritual awakening. His writings influenced thinkers such as Mahatma Gandhi, who adopted many of his ideas into political action. Tolstoy’s contribution lies in his ability to humanize history, to show that behind every war are countless personal stories of loss, love, and survival."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.0828,0.1702,-0.9759]},{"id":"woolf","name":"Virginia Woolf","dates":"January 25, 1882 – March 28, 1941","u":0.0833,"band":3,"teaser":"War does not end when the fighting stops; it lingers in minds, memories, and societies.","text":["Virginia Woolf lived through a period marked by the upheaval of World War I and the looming threat of another global conflict. As a central figure in modernist literature, she sought to capture the inner lives of her characters, often reflecting the psychological toll of a world shaped by violence. In works such as <em>Mrs. Dalloway</em>, the trauma of war is present not in battle scenes but in the fractured consciousness of returning soldiers and the quiet struggles of everyday life.","Woolf was also a committed feminist, challenging the structures that limited women’s voices and opportunities. In essays like <em>Three Guineas</em>, she directly addressed the relationship between patriarchy, education, and war, arguing that the same systems that oppress women also perpetuate conflict. Her work reminds us that war does not end when the fighting stops; it lingers in minds, memories, and societies."],"kind":"person","p":[0.3677,0.0301,-0.9681]},{"id":"shaw","name":"George Bernard Shaw","dates":"July 26, 1856 – November 2, 1950","u":0.1667,"band":3,"teaser":"Conflict often persists not because of necessity, but because of unquestioned belief.","text":["George Bernard Shaw used words as instruments of disruption. Born in Dublin and later working in London, he became one of the most influential playwrights of his time, known for works such as <em>Man and Superman</em> and <em>Pygmalion</em>. Shaw was not content to entertain. He challenged audiences to confront uncomfortable truths about class, politics, and the illusions that sustain society.","During World War I, Shaw’s outspoken criticism of both sides made him a controversial figure. He rejected blind patriotism and questioned the narratives that justified mass slaughter. His essays and plays exposed the absurdities of war and the systems that perpetuate it. Shaw’s contribution lies in his refusal to accept easy answers. He reminds us that conflict often persists not because of necessity, but because of unquestioned belief."],"kind":"person","p":[0.8164,-0.4236,-0.3761]},{"id":"suttner","name":"Bertha von Suttner","dates":"June 9, 1843 – June 21, 1914","u":0.25,"band":3,"teaser":"A voice for peace unheard in time.","text":["Bertha von Suttner was born into aristocracy, yet her life’s work was dedicated to dismantling the culture of war that surrounded her. Her novel <em>Lay Down Your Arms!</em> published in 1889, became a powerful call for peace, depicting the devastating human cost of conflict. At a time when militarism was widely accepted, her voice stood in stark opposition.","She played a crucial role in the early peace movement, organizing congresses and advocating for international arbitration. Her friendship with Alfred Nobel influenced the creation of the Nobel Peace Prize, which she herself received in 1905. Tragically, she died just weeks before the outbreak of World War I, a conflict she had long warned against. Her life feels almost prophetic, a voice for peace unheard in time."],"kind":"person","p":[0.6573,-0.413,0.622]},{"id":"lennon","name":"John Lennon","dates":"October 9, 1940 – December 8, 1980","u":0.3333,"band":3,"teaser":"Peace can be both a dream and a discipline.","text":["John Lennon rose to global fame as a member of The Beatles, but his later life was marked by a deep commitment to peace activism. Alongside Yoko Ono, he staged “bed-ins” and used his platform to protest the Vietnam War. Songs like <em>Imagine</em> became anthems for a generation seeking alternatives to conflict, envisioning a world without borders or divisions.","Lennon’s activism was unconventional, blending art, media, and protest into a single expression. He understood that culture could influence politics, that a song could carry a message as powerfully as a speech. His life was cut short by violence, a stark contrast to the ideals he championed. Lennon stands as a reminder that peace can be both a dream and a discipline."],"kind":"person","p":[0.8349,0.0792,0.5248]},{"id":"yusuf","name":"Yusuf Islam","dates":"July 21, 1948 – Present","u":0.4167,"band":3,"teaser":"The role of culture and spirituality in shaping ideas of peace.","text":["Born Steven Demetre Georgiou and known globally as Cat Stevens, Yusuf Islam rose to fame in the late 1960s and 1970s with songs that often explored themes of introspection, peace, and spiritual searching. At the height of his career, he stepped away from the music industry after converting to Islam, dedicating himself to education and humanitarian work.","His life reflects a journey from fame to faith, from public performance to personal conviction. In later years, he returned to music, blending his artistic voice with messages of unity and compassion. His inclusion in this installation highlights the role of culture and spirituality in shaping ideas of peace, offering a contrast to figures whose influence was exercised through political or military means."],"kind":"person","p":[1.0253,0.0834,-0.114]},{"id":"teresa","name":"Mother Teresa","dates":"August 26, 1910 – September 5, 1997","u":0.5,"band":3,"teaser":"Peace is not only negotiated in treaties or won in battles, but practiced in acts of care and dignity.","text":["Born in Skopje and later working in India, Mother Teresa devoted her life to serving the poorest of the poor. Through the Missionaries of Charity, she established homes for the sick, the dying, and the abandoned. Her work was not political in the traditional sense, yet it addressed the human suffering that often follows in the wake of conflict and inequality.","Awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1979, she became a global symbol of compassion. Her life suggests that peace is not only negotiated in treaties or won in battles, but practiced in acts of care and dignity. In the quiet spaces where she worked, peace took on a deeply personal form."],"kind":"person","p":[0.1877,-0.5262,-0.8359]},{"id":"reagan","name":"Ronald Reagan","dates":"February 6, 1911 – June 5, 2004","u":0.5833,"band":3,"teaser":"Peace can emerge not only from idealism but from pragmatic engagement between adversaries.","text":["Ronald Reagan entered the presidency during the height of the Cold War, a period defined not by direct conflict between superpowers but by the constant threat of nuclear annihilation. Initially, his administration took a hardline stance against the Soviet Union, increasing military spending and referring to it as an “evil empire.” Yet as his presidency progressed, Reagan shifted toward diplomacy, engaging directly with Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev.","Their meetings, including the Reykjavik Summit in 1986, laid the groundwork for arms reduction agreements that would ease global tensions. Reagan’s role in the eventual end of the Cold War reflects the complex interplay between strength and negotiation. His presidency illustrates how peace can emerge not only from idealism but from pragmatic engagement between adversaries."],"kind":"person","p":[0.7196,-0.6626,0.1954]},{"id":"bush","name":"George W. Bush","dates":"July 6, 1946 – Present","u":0.6667,"band":3,"teaser":"The challenges of responding to unconventional threats in a globalized world.","text":["George W. Bush’s presidency was defined by the events of September 11, 2001, when terrorist attacks reshaped the United States and its role in the world. In response, his administration launched the War on Terror, including military operations in Afghanistan to dismantle al-Qaeda and later the invasion of Iraq in 2003. These actions were framed as efforts to combat terrorism and promote security, yet they also led to prolonged conflict and debate over their consequences.","Bush’s leadership illustrates the challenges of responding to unconventional threats in a globalized world. His decisions continue to influence international relations and discussions of war, security, and the balance between action and restraint."],"kind":"person","p":[0.6539,0.1013,-0.8097]},{"id":"binladen","name":"Osama bin Laden","dates":"March 10, 1957 – May 2, 2011","u":0.75,"band":3,"teaser":"Individuals can reshape global dynamics through acts of violence that transcend borders.","text":["Osama bin Laden was born into a wealthy Saudi family but became radicalized by geopolitical conflicts in the Middle East. He founded al-Qaeda in the late 1980s, initially as a network supporting fighters in Afghanistan. Over time, it evolved into a global organization dedicated to opposing Western influence through violent means. The attacks of September 11, 2001, which targeted the United States, marked a turning point in modern history, leading to wars in Afghanistan and Iraq.","Bin Laden’s actions exemplify a form of warfare that operates outside traditional battlefields, relying on asymmetry and ideology. His death in 2011 during a U.S. operation did not end the conflicts he helped ignite. His legacy is one of disruption and fear, demonstrating how individuals can reshape global dynamics through acts of violence that transcend borders."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.3173,-0.5189,-0.801]},{"id":"saddam","name":"Saddam Hussein","dates":"April 28, 1937 – December 30, 2006","u":0.8333,"band":3,"teaser":"The fragility of power built on coercion, and the long shadows such leadership casts.","text":["Saddam Hussein rose from a modest background in Tikrit to become one of the most feared rulers of the late twentieth century. He consolidated power in Iraq in 1979, ruling through a combination of political maneuvering, surveillance, and brutal repression. His regime was marked by the Iran–Iraq War from 1980 to 1988, a prolonged and devastating conflict that cost hundreds of thousands of lives and reshaped the region’s balance of power. Later, in 1990, his invasion of Kuwait triggered the Gulf War, bringing a U.S.-led coalition into direct conflict with Iraq.","Internally, Saddam maintained control through fear, including the use of chemical weapons against Kurdish populations in Halabja. His rule exemplifies the use of violence as both a domestic and international tool. Ultimately, his regime fell during the 2003 U.S. invasion of Iraq under George W. Bush. Saddam’s story reflects the fragility of power built on coercion and the long shadows such leadership casts over a nation."],"kind":"person","p":[0.7577,-0.4506,0.435]},{"id":"noriega","name":"Manuel Noriega","dates":"February 11, 1934 – May 29, 2017","u":0.9167,"band":3,"teaser":"Alliances can shift quickly and power can disappear almost overnight.","text":["Manuel Noriega rose through the ranks of Panama’s military and intelligence services to become the country’s de facto ruler during the 1980s. For years, he maintained complex relationships with foreign governments, particularly the United States, positioning himself as both ally and opportunist during the Cold War. Behind the scenes, however, allegations of corruption, drug trafficking, and political repression increasingly defined his regime.","Tensions with the United States escalated dramatically in 1989 when President George H. W. Bush ordered the invasion of Panama in Operation Just Cause. The military intervention led to Noriega’s capture and trial in the United States. His downfall illustrated the volatile intersections of power, foreign influence, and military intervention in the modern era. Noriega’s story reflects how smaller nations often become entangled in larger geopolitical struggles, where alliances can shift quickly and power can disappear almost overnight."],"kind":"person","p":[0.4456,-0.6893,0.564]},{"id":"putin","name":"Vladimir Putin","dates":"October 7, 1952 – Present","u":0.0833,"band":1,"teaser":"The persistence of geopolitical struggle in a modern world that often claims to seek peace.","text":["Vladimir Putin emerged from the ranks of the KGB to become one of the most influential leaders of the twenty-first century. Since first assuming the presidency in 2000, he has worked to reassert Russia’s power on the global stage. His leadership has been marked by centralization of authority, control over political opposition, and a willingness to use military force beyond Russia’s borders.","Conflicts in Chechnya, Georgia, Crimea, and Ukraine define much of his tenure. The invasion of Ukraine in 2022 stands as a pivotal moment, reshaping international alliances and reviving tensions reminiscent of the Cold War. Putin’s role reflects the persistence of geopolitical struggle in a modern world that often claims to seek peace."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.7649,-0.012,0.6395]},{"id":"zelenskyy","name":"Volodymyr Zelenskyy","dates":"January 25, 1978 – Present","u":0.5833,"band":1,"teaser":"He represents the modern face of leadership under siege.","text":["Volodymyr Zelenskyy’s path to leadership was anything but conventional. A former actor and comedian, he rose to prominence playing a fictional president before becoming the real one in 2019. His presidency was transformed by the Russian invasion of Ukraine in 2022, led by Vladimir Putin. Faced with the collapse of his nation’s security, Zelenskyy chose to remain in Kyiv, rejecting offers of evacuation with the now-famous assertion that he needed ammunition, not a ride.","Under his leadership, Ukraine mounted a determined resistance against a larger military force. Zelenskyy’s nightly addresses and international appeals echoed the wartime leadership of figures like Winston Churchill, blending communication with strategy. His role continues to evolve, balancing the immediate demands of war with the long-term hope for sovereignty and peace. He represents the modern face of leadership under siege."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.207,-0.3463,0.8956]},{"id":"trump","name":"Donald Trump","dates":"June 14, 1946 – Present","u":0.1667,"band":1,"teaser":"Where media, personality, and politics intersect in ways that influence both conflict and the pursuit of stability.","text":["Donald Trump’s presidency reflects a period of deep division and transformation within the United States. Serving first as the 45th president and later returning as the 47th, his leadership has been marked by a direct and often confrontational style. His approach to foreign policy emphasized national interests, including renegotiating trade agreements and engaging in high-profile diplomacy with North Korea.","His tenure also included moments of tension, both domestically and internationally, illustrating how leadership can shape not only policy but the tone of public discourse. Trump’s role in <em>The Art of War and Peace</em> represents the evolving nature of power in the modern era, where media, personality, and politics intersect in ways that influence both conflict and the pursuit of stability."],"kind":"person","p":[0.0675,-0.4793,0.8477]},{"id":"kim","name":"Kim Jong Un","dates":"c. 1982–1984 – Present","u":0.3333,"band":1,"teaser":"War is often unspoken but ever-present, held in balance by the fear of its consequences.","text":["Kim Jong Un inherited leadership of North Korea in 2011, continuing a dynastic rule established by his grandfather. His tenure has been defined by the development of nuclear weapons and missile programs, positioning his nation at the center of global tension. Through carefully staged displays of military strength, he has maintained a posture of deterrence, where the threat of war becomes a tool of survival.","At the same time, his leadership has included moments of diplomacy, including summits with international leaders. These gestures, however, exist alongside strict internal control and isolation. Kim’s role reflects a modern form of power, where war is often unspoken but ever-present, held in balance by the fear of its consequences."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.7027,-0.0038,-0.6951]},{"id":"netanyahu","name":"Benjamin Netanyahu","dates":"October 21, 1949 – Present","u":0.4167,"band":1,"teaser":"A tension that remains unresolved and deeply human.","text":["Benjamin Netanyahu has been a defining figure in Israeli politics for decades, serving multiple terms as Prime Minister. His career has unfolded against the backdrop of one of the most enduring and complex conflicts in modern history, the Israeli–Palestinian struggle. A former soldier and diplomat, Netanyahu has consistently emphasized security, particularly in relation to terrorism and regional threats such as Iran.","His leadership has seen periods of negotiation as well as escalation, including conflicts in Gaza and shifting diplomatic relations in the Middle East. Agreements such as the Abraham Accords marked significant changes in regional alliances, even as tensions with Palestinians persist. Netanyahu’s role reflects the difficulty of balancing national security with the pursuit of lasting peace, a tension that remains unresolved and deeply human."],"kind":"person","p":[-0.5155,-0.1497,0.8469]},{"id":"kitchener","name":"Lord Kitchener Wants You","dates":"1914","u":0.6667,"band":1,"teaser":"Persuasion and imagery can mobilize entire populations.","text":["Created by artist Alfred Leete, this image of Herbert Kitchener became one of the most recognizable symbols of World War I. The poster shows Kitchener pointing directly at the viewer, his gaze unwavering, accompanied by the words “WANTS YOU.” It was more than an advertisement. It was a call to identity, duty, and participation in a global conflict.","The power of the image lies in its simplicity. It transforms war from a distant event into a personal obligation. Millions of men were encouraged to enlist through its influence and through similar campaigns it inspired. In this way, the poster represents a shift in how war was fought, not only on battlefields, but in the minds of civilians. It stands as a reminder that persuasion and imagery can mobilize entire populations, turning individuals into participants in something far larger than themselves."],"kind":"icon","p":[0.693,-0.0671,0.7125]},{"id":"unclesam","name":"Uncle Sam","dates":"Early 19th century origin, popularized 1917","u":0.8333,"band":1,"teaser":"A bridge between people and power.","text":["Uncle Sam emerged as a symbolic figure representing the United States, evolving over time into a powerful image of national identity. During World War I, artist James Montgomery Flagg created the famous recruitment poster in which Uncle Sam points outward, echoing the direct appeal of Lord Kitchener’s image. The message was clear. The nation was calling, and the responsibility belonged to each individual.","Unlike a political leader or general, Uncle Sam is an idea made visible. He embodies patriotism, authority, and collective identity. His image has been used to inspire, persuade, and at times critique the actions of the government he represents. In the context of <em>The Art of War and Peace</em>, he stands as a bridge between people and power, showing how nations themselves become actors in the story of war and peace."],"kind":"icon","p":[-0.579,-0.3606,0.7312]},{"id":"rosie","name":"Rosie the Riveter","dates":"1940s wartime symbol","u":0.9167,"band":1,"teaser":"Wars are fought not only by soldiers on battlefields, but by civilians whose labor makes those battles possible.","text":["Rosie the Riveter emerged during World War II as a powerful symbol of the American home front. With millions of men serving overseas, women entered factories, shipyards, and industrial jobs in unprecedented numbers. Through posters, songs, and magazine covers, Rosie became the face of that movement, most famously depicted with a rolled sleeve beneath the words “We Can Do It!” Though often associated with a single image created by J. Howard Miller, Rosie represented countless real women whose labor sustained wartime production.","Her significance extends beyond the war itself. Rosie challenged traditional ideas about gender and work, proving that women could perform physically demanding industrial jobs once reserved almost exclusively for men. While many women were pressured to leave those positions after the war ended, the cultural shift could not be fully reversed. Rosie represents a different dimension of conflict, not combat itself, but the mobilization of society behind it. She reminds us that wars are fought not only by soldiers on battlefields, but also by civilians whose labor, sacrifice, and resilience make those battles possible."],"kind":"icon","p":[0.8711,-0.5168,0.0094]}],"throng":[{"id":"liberty","name":"Liberty Leading the People","img":"liberty_s","ref":{"img":"liberty","ar":1.25},"layout":"row","caps":[{"pos":"Left","t":"<em>Liberty Leading the People, The Art of War and Peace</em>, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"pos":"Right","t":"<em>Liberty Leading the People</em>, Eugène Delacroix, Oil on canvas, 102.4″ × 128″, 1830, Louvre, Paris"}],"text":["Painted in response to the July Revolution of 1830 in France, <em>Liberty Leading the People</em> captures a nation in the turbulent act of redefining itself. Rather than portraying a specific battle, Delacroix immortalized the spirit of revolution through the allegorical figure of Liberty, barefoot and fearless, carrying the French tricolor while leading citizens from every social class over the fallen. Students, workers, and the bourgeoisie advance together, suggesting that the pursuit of freedom belongs to everyone.","Although Delacroix did not fight in the revolution himself, he believed art could preserve the emotional truth of history. Painted during the height of Romanticism, when artists valued passion, heroism, and individual expression over classical restraint, the work celebrates courage while acknowledging its terrible cost. It reminds us that revolutions are born from hope, yet must pass through sacrifice. In <em>The Art of War and Peace</em>, it represents determination, unity, and humanity's enduring willingness to risk everything for liberty."],"teaser":"Determination, unity, and humanity’s enduring willingness to risk everything for liberty.","excerpt":"Painted in response to the July Revolution of 1830 in France, <em>Liberty Leading the People</em> captures a nation in the turbulent act of redefining itself…","ar":0.7238,"p":[-0.7799,0.0787,0.1225],"view":[-0.655,0.5,2.8],"credit":"Liberty Leading the People, The Art of War and Peace, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"id":"thirdofmay","name":"The Third of May","img":"thirdofmay_s","ref":{"img":"thirdofmay","ar":1.2953},"layout":"row","caps":[{"pos":"Left","t":"<em>The Third of May, The Art of War and Peace</em>, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"pos":"Right","t":"<em>The Third of May</em>, Francisco Goya, Oil on canvas, 106″ × 137″, 1814, Museo del Prado, Madrid"}],"text":["Few paintings capture the horror of war with greater honesty than Francisco Goya's <em>The Third of May 1808</em>. Painted after Napoleon's occupation of Spain, it commemorates the execution of Spanish civilians by French soldiers following an uprising in Madrid. The firing squad appears faceless and mechanical, while the central figure, dressed in white with arms spread wide, becomes a universal symbol of innocence confronting violence.","Goya had witnessed the brutality of the Peninsular War firsthand. Rather than glorifying military victory, he exposed war's cruelty and its devastating human consequences. This marked a profound departure from traditional history painting, which often celebrated heroic triumphs. Instead, Goya gave dignity to ordinary victims, forever changing how artists portrayed conflict. His work reminds us that behind every victory lie countless personal tragedies, many of them forgotten by history but preserved through art."],"teaser":"Behind every victory lie countless personal tragedies, many of them forgotten by history but preserved through art.","excerpt":"Few paintings capture the horror of war with greater honesty than Francisco Goya's <em>The Third of May 1808</em>. Painted after Napoleon's occupation of…","ar":1.0624,"p":[-0.4161,0.2182,0.1268],"view":[-0.655,0.5,2.8],"credit":"The Third of May, The Art of War and Peace, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"id":"gassed","name":"Gassed","img":"gassed_s","ref":{"img":"gassed","ar":2.6726},"layout":"stack","caps":[{"pos":"Top","t":"<em>Gassed: The Art of War and Peace</em>, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"pos":"Bottom","t":"<em>Gassed</em>, John Singer Sargent, Oil on canvas, 7.6′ × 20′, 1919, Imperial War Museum, London"}],"text":["Commissioned by the British government after World War I, <em>Gassed</em> depicts a line of blinded soldiers carefully guiding one another after a mustard gas attack. Sargent, renowned before the war for elegant society portraits, traveled to the Western Front expecting to paint scenes of military accomplishment. Instead, he encountered industrialized warfare unlike anything the world had known.","The painting is remarkable for its quiet restraint. There are no dramatic charges or heroic victories, only exhausted young men helping one another across a devastated landscape while, in the distance, another football game continues among troops, an unsettling reminder of humanity's ability to seek normalcy amid unimaginable suffering. Created in the aftermath of one of history's deadliest conflicts, <em>Gassed</em> reflects a world disillusioned by mechanized war. It transforms endurance and compassion into acts of heroism, reminding us that survival itself can be an extraordinary triumph."],"teaser":"It transforms endurance and compassion into acts of heroism, reminding us that survival itself can be an extraordinary triumph.","excerpt":"Commissioned by the British government after World War I, <em>Gassed</em> depicts a line of blinded soldiers carefully guiding one another after a mustard gas…","ar":2.4948,"p":[0.0085,0.2246,-0.6049],"view":[0.095,0.5,2.8],"credit":"Gassed: The Art of War and Peace, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"id":"kissing","name":"The Kissing Sailor – V‑J Day in Times Square","img":"kissing_s","ref":{"img":"kissing","ar":0.6684},"layout":"row","caps":[{"pos":"Left","t":"<em>The Kissing Sailor: The Art of War and Peace</em>, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"pos":"Right","t":"<em>The Kissing Sailor – V-J Day in Times Square</em>, Alfred Eisenstaedt, Photograph, 1945, Life Magazine"}],"text":["Taken on August 14, 1945, as Americans celebrated Victory over Japan Day, Alfred Eisenstaedt's photograph captured what became one of the most recognizable images of the twentieth century. A jubilant sailor embraces and kisses a woman dressed in white in the middle of Times Square, surrounded by crowds celebrating the end of World War II. To millions, the image symbolized relief after years of sacrifice, rationing, loss, and uncertainty. It embodied the overwhelming joy of peace finally returning.","Over time, however, the photograph has taken on more complex meaning. The identities of those depicted were debated for decades, and modern discussions have raised questions about consent and the realities behind spontaneous wartime celebration. This evolution reflects an important truth about history itself. Images, like memories, can change as society changes. Within <em>The Art of War and Peace</em>, the photograph represents both the euphoria of victory and the evolving ways we interpret the past. Peace is not only declared. It is remembered, questioned, and continually reexamined."],"teaser":"Peace is not only declared. It is remembered, questioned, and continually reexamined.","excerpt":"Taken on August 14, 1945, as Americans celebrated Victory over Japan Day, Alfred Eisenstaedt's photograph captured what became one of the most…","ar":0.7319,"p":[0.2436,0.0812,0.3834],"view":[0.395,0.5,2.8],"credit":"The Kissing Sailor: The Art of War and Peace, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"id":"terror","name":"The Terror of War","img":"terror_s","ref":{"img":"terror","ar":1.6835},"layout":"row","caps":[{"pos":"Left","t":"<em>The Terror of War</em>, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"pos":"Right","t":"<em>The Terror of War – Phan Thị Kim Phúc</em>, Nick Ut, Photograph, 1972"}],"text":["Perhaps no single photograph altered public perception of a war more profoundly than <em>The Terror of War</em>, commonly known as <em>Napalm Girl</em>. Taken by Associated Press photographer Nick Ut during the Vietnam War, it shows nine-year-old Phan Thị Kim Phúc running naked down a road after a South Vietnamese napalm strike severely burned her body. The image strips away politics, ideology, and military strategy, confronting viewers with the suffering of a single child caught in the machinery of war.","Nick Ut immediately put down his camera to help Kim Phúc, rushing her to a hospital where doctors initially believed she would not survive. She endured years of surgeries and eventually became a UNESCO Goodwill Ambassador and international advocate for peace and reconciliation. The photograph helped shift global opinion about the Vietnam War by making its human cost impossible to ignore. In this sculpture, it stands as perhaps the most powerful reminder that wars are ultimately measured not by territory gained or lost, but by the lives forever changed in their wake."],"teaser":"Wars are ultimately measured not by territory gained or lost, but by the lives forever changed in their wake.","excerpt":"Perhaps no single photograph altered public perception of a war more profoundly than <em>The Terror of War</em>, commonly known as <em>Napalm Girl</em>. Taken by…","ar":0.7492,"p":[0.088,-0.1289,0.1072],"view":[0.095,0.5,2.8],"credit":"The Terror of War, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"},{"id":"elephant","name":"The Elephant in Battle","img":"elephant_s","layout":"single","subtitle":"Inspired by the War Elephants of Carthage and Hannibal","caps":[{"pos":"","t":"<em>The Elephant in Battle, The Art of War and Peace</em>, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"}],"text":["The elephant in <em>The Art of War and Peace</em> draws from one of the extraordinary chapters of ancient warfare. During the Second Punic War, the Carthaginian general Hannibal crossed the Alps into Italy with an army that reportedly included 37 war elephants. Their sheer size and presence were weapons in themselves, intended to terrify soldiers and horses, disrupt formations, and demonstrate the immense reach and resources of Carthage. At the Battle of Zama in 202 BC, Hannibal again deployed elephants against the Roman army, commanded by Scipio Africanus. By then, however, the Romans had learned how to counter them, creating openings through their ranks and attacking the animals from the sides. Some elephants panicked and turned back toward their own forces, becoming victims of the chaos they had been brought to create.","In the sculpture, the dying elephant becomes more than a reference to ancient warfare. It represents the innocent lives, human and animal, drawn into conflicts they did not create. An animal once used as an instrument of power becomes a casualty of that same power. Its presence connects the ancient world to the present, reminding us that although weapons and strategies evolve, the human cost of war remains remarkably constant."],"teaser":"An animal once used as an instrument of power becomes a casualty of that same power.","excerpt":"The elephant in <em>The Art of War and Peace</em> draws from one of the extraordinary chapters of ancient warfare. During the Second Punic War, the…","ar":0.911,"p":[0.4777,0.1256,0.3057],"view":[0.695,0.5,2.8],"credit":"The Elephant in Battle, The Art of War and Peace, Foam sculpture detail, 2027"}],"faiths":{"id":"faiths","name":"Religious Symbols and Traditions","dates":"Christianity, Judaism, Islam, Hinduism, Buddhism, Shinto, Taoism, Sikhism, Pastafarianism","credit":"Christianity, Judaism, Islam, Hinduism, Buddhism, Shinto, Taoism, Sikhism, Pastafarianism","teaser":"Faith can inspire acts of great kindness and profound violence. It can divide or unite.","text":["These traditions are not individuals, yet they carry immense influence across history. Each offers teachings that speak to peace, compassion, and moral responsibility, while also existing within histories that include conflict and division. Symbols such as the cross, the crescent, the lotus, and others represent belief systems that have guided billions of lives.","In <em>The Art of War and Peace</em>, these symbols serve as a unifying layer beneath the figures themselves. They remind the viewer that beyond leaders and events, there are deeper currents shaping human behavior. Faith can inspire acts of great kindness and profound violence. It can divide or unite. Like war and peace themselves, it reflects the complexity of the human condition."]},"extra":{"hitler":[[-0.9951,0.0697,-0.2538]],"garibaldi":[[-0.6312,-0.3467,-0.6988]],"lennon":[[-0.7312,-0.5402,-0.3962]]}};
